{{also|Scoop|-scoop}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|scope}}, {{m|enm|schoupe}}, a borrowing from {{der|en|dum|scoep}}, {{m|dum|scuep}}, {{m|dum|schope}}, {{m|dum|schoepe|t=bucket for bailing water}} and {{der|en|dum|schoppe}}, {{m|dum|scoppe}}, {{m|dum|schuppe}} {{gloss|"a scoop, shovel"; > Modern {{cog|nl|schop|t=spade}}}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*skuppǭ}}, {{m|gem-pro|*skuppijǭ}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*(s)kep-|t=to cut, to scrape, to hack}}.<ref>http://www.etymonline.com/index.php?term=scoop</ref>. Cognate with {{cog|ofs|skuppe|t=shovel}}, {{cog|gml|schōpe|t=scoop, shovel}}, {{cog|nds-de|Schüppe}}, {{m|nds-de|Schüpp|t=shovel}}, {{cog|de|Schüppe}}, {{m|de|Schippe|t=shovel, spade}}. Related to {{cog|en|shovel}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|sko͞op}}, {{IPA|/skuːp/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-scoop.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|uːp|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun}}

# Any cup- or bowl-shaped tool, usually with a [[handle]], used to lift and move loose or soft solid material.
#: ''She kept a '''scoop''' in the dog food.''
# The [[amount]] or [[volume]] of loose or solid material held by a particular scoop.
#: ''Use one '''scoop''' of coffee for each pot.''
#: ''I'll have one '''scoop''' of chocolate ice-cream.''
# The act of scooping, or taking with a scoop or ladle; a motion with a scoop, as in dipping or shovelling.
# A [[story]] or [[fact]]; especially, news learned and reported before anyone else.
#: ''He listened carefully, in hopes of getting the [[scoop]] on the debate.''
# {{lb|en|automotive}} An opening in a [[hood]]/[[bonnet]] or other [[body]] panel to admit air, usually for cooling the [[engine]].
# The digging attachment on a [[front-end loader]].
# A place hollowed out; a basinlike cavity; a hollow.
#* J. R. Drake
#*: Some had lain in the '''scoop''' of the rock.
# A spoon-shaped [[surgical]] instrument, used in extracting certain substances or foreign bodies.
# A special spinal board used by [[EMS|emergency medical service]] staff that divides laterally to literally scoop up patients.
# A sweep; a stroke; a swoop.

